    <!-- 07 Brief Structure -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="brief">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Brief Structure</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How to brief creators for branded content</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">A good brief constrains the message, not the creative execution</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Include: product story, key messages, hooks, tone, format flexibility</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Over-scripting kills authenticity; under-briefing misses the message</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The brief is the single document that determines whether branded content will feel authentic or forced. A well-structured brief gives the creator everything they need to tell the brand's story while leaving room for their own creative voice. A poorly structured brief &mdash; either too rigid or too vague &mdash; produces content that either sounds scripted or misses the mark entirely.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The seven elements of a branded content brief</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Product story.</strong> What is the product? What problem does it solve? Why should the viewer care? Give the creator enough context to understand the product genuinely &mdash; not just the marketing pitch, but the real value proposition. If possible, let the creator use the product before filming.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Key messages.</strong> List 2&ndash;3 messages that must appear in the content. These are non-negotiable talking points: the product name, the core benefit, and a call to action. Keep the list short. Every additional mandatory message reduces the creator's creative freedom and makes the content feel more scripted.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Hook suggestions.</strong> Provide 3&ndash;5 hook ideas the creator can use or adapt. Hooks are the first 1&ndash;3 seconds of the video &mdash; the part that determines whether someone watches or scrolls. Good hooks are specific, curiosity-driven, or pattern-interrupting. Bad hooks are generic ("You need to see this product").</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Tone and style.</strong> Describe the desired tone: conversational, educational, humorous, aspirational. Reference specific creators or posts that exemplify the tone you want. Avoid abstract descriptors like "authentic" or "relatable" &mdash; every brand says that. Show, don't tell.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Format flexibility.</strong> Specify which formats are acceptable: talking head, get-ready-with-me, tutorial, before/after, day-in-my-life, unboxing, comparison. If possible, let the creator choose their strongest format rather than prescribing one. The best content comes from formats the creator already knows how to execute well.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Do's and don'ts.</strong> Explicit constraints: do mention the website URL, don't make medical claims, do show the product in use, don't mention competitors by name. These guardrails prevent compliance issues and off-brand content without micromanaging the creative process.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Deliverables and timeline.</strong> Number of videos, aspect ratio, minimum and maximum duration, delivery format (native post vs. raw footage), revision policy, and deadline. Be specific: "2 videos, 9:16, 30&ndash;60 seconds each, delivered as .mp4 by [date]."</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Common briefing mistakes</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>Word-for-word scripts</strong> &mdash; kill authenticity. The audience can tell when someone is reading. Provide talking points, not a teleprompter script.</li>
          <li><strong>Too many mandatory messages</strong> &mdash; cramming 6+ talking points into a 30-second video makes every one of them weaker. Pick 2&ndash;3 and let the creator integrate them naturally.</li>
          <li><strong>No visual references</strong> &mdash; describing the desired content in text alone leads to misalignment. Link to example videos that capture the tone, pacing, and style you want.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignoring the creator's strengths</strong> &mdash; if a creator excels at humor and you brief them for a serious testimonial, the result will be awkward. Cast for the format you need, or adapt the brief to the creator's strengths.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Legal Requirements -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="legal">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Legal</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Legal requirements and disclosure rules</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">FTC requires "clear and conspicuous" disclosure of material connections</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Each platform has its own built-in disclosure tools &mdash; use them</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">International rules vary: UK (ASA), EU (UCPD), and others have their own standards</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Branded content advertising is subject to disclosure requirements in every major market. The core principle is consistent across jurisdictions: when there is a "material connection" between a creator and a brand (payment, free product, commission, or any other compensation), that connection must be disclosed clearly to the audience. Failure to disclose is a legal violation that can result in fines, enforcement actions, and reputational damage for both the brand and the creator.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">United States: FTC guidelines</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The Federal Trade Commission requires that endorsements reflect the honest opinion of the endorser and that material connections be disclosed "clearly and conspicuously." In practice, this means: the disclosure must be hard to miss (not buried in hashtags or at the end of a long caption), in the same language as the content, and unambiguous. "#ad" or "Paid partnership with [brand]" at the beginning of the caption meets the standard. "#sp" buried among 30 other hashtags does not. The FTC has actively pursued enforcement actions against both brands and creators for inadequate disclosure.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Platform-specific disclosure tools</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Platform</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Built-in tool</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Label shown to audience</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">TikTok</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Branded content toggle in post settings</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">"Paid Partnership"</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Instagram / Facebook</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Branded content tag (select brand partner)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">"Paid partnership with [brand name]"</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">YouTube</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Paid promotion checkbox in video details</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">"Includes paid promotion"</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Pinterest</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Paid partnership label</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">"Paid partnership"</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Using the platform's built-in disclosure tool is the minimum requirement, not a substitute for clear text disclosure. Best practice is to use both: activate the platform's branded content toggle and include "#ad" or "Paid partnership" in the caption or verbal disclosure within the first few seconds of the video.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">International requirements</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">In the UK, the Advertising Standards Authority (ASA) and Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) require clear labeling of paid content. The standard labels are "#ad" or "AD" &mdash; not "#gifted," "#collab," or "#spon." The EU's Unfair Commercial Practices Directive (UCPD) provides a framework that member states implement individually, but the principle is the same: undisclosed commercial content is considered misleading. Australia (AANA), Canada (Ad Standards), and Brazil (CONAR) all have equivalent rules with varying levels of enforcement.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When running branded content ads across multiple markets, the safest approach is to comply with the strictest applicable standard. In practice, this means: use the platform's disclosure tool, include "#ad" prominently in the caption, and include a verbal disclosure in the video when possible. OKAD builds compliance checks into every branded content brief to ensure creators meet all applicable requirements before content is published or promoted.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 Measurement and ROI -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="measurement">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Measurement</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Measuring branded content performance</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Direct response metrics: CTR, CPA, ROAS, conversion rate</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Brand metrics: lift studies, recall, consideration, favorability</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Incrementality testing isolates the true impact of branded content</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Measuring branded content ads requires tracking both direct response performance and brand impact. Unlike standard display or search ads, branded content often delivers value across the funnel simultaneously &mdash; driving immediate clicks while building long-term brand awareness. A measurement framework that only captures one dimension will undervalue the investment.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Direct response metrics</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">These are the standard performance marketing metrics applied to branded content ads. They measure whether the ad is driving immediate, measurable actions.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Metric</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">What it measures</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Benchmark range</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CTR (click-through rate)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">% of viewers who click the CTA</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">0.8&ndash;2.5% (branded content typically 1.5&ndash;3x standard ads)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CPA (cost per acquisition)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Cost to acquire a customer or lead</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Varies by vertical; aim for 20&ndash;40% lower than standard ads</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">ROAS (return on ad spend)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Revenue generated per dollar of ad spend</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Category-dependent; 3&ndash;8x for e-commerce, 1&ndash;3x for SaaS</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CPM (cost per 1,000 impressions)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Efficiency of media spend</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">$5&ndash;$15 (Spark Ads), $8&ndash;$25 (Meta), $10&ndash;$30 (YouTube)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Engagement rate</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Likes + comments + shares / impressions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">2&ndash;5x higher than standard ad creatives</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Brand lift studies</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Brand lift studies measure the impact of branded content on awareness, recall, consideration, and purchase intent. Both TikTok and Meta offer built-in brand lift study tools that compare a test group (exposed to the ad) against a control group (not exposed). These studies answer questions that direct response metrics cannot: "Did this campaign make people more aware of the brand?" and "Did it change their likelihood to purchase?" Brand lift is particularly important for branded content because its effects often extend beyond the immediate click.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Incrementality testing</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Incrementality testing is the gold standard for measuring the true impact of branded content ads. It isolates what the ad campaign caused vs. what would have happened without it. The standard approach is a geographic holdout test: run branded content ads in some regions but not others, then compare conversion rates between the groups. This eliminates attribution noise (users who would have converted anyway, cross-channel effects) and provides a clean measure of incremental value. OKAD recommends running incrementality tests quarterly for any branded content program with monthly spend above $10K.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Creative-level analysis</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Beyond campaign-level metrics, branded content programs should track performance at the individual creative level. Which creator's content drives the lowest CPA? Which hook style generates the highest CTR? Which format (talking head vs. tutorial vs. unboxing) delivers the best ROAS? This creative-level data feeds back into the <a href="/guide" class="text-accent hover:underline">creative testing</a> process and informs future briefs, creator selection, and format decisions. Over time, a brand builds a data-driven understanding of what branded content works for their specific product and audience.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

